==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|envoke}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|invoken}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|invoquer}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|invocō|invocāre|to call upon}}, itself from {{prefix|in-|vocare|t2=to call|lang=la|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|in'vōk}}, {{IPA|/ɪnˈvoʊk/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|invok}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[call]] upon (a person, especially a [[god]]) for [[help]], [[assistance]] or [[guidance]].
#* {{quote-book|passage=After marriage, the man had anciently (but this was anterior to Christianity) the power of life and death over his wife. She could '''invoke''' no law against him; he was her sole tribunal and law.|title=[[S:The Subjection of Women|The Subjection of Women]]|author=John Stuart Mill|year=1869}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=The acquisition of a useless part can hardly be said to raise an organism in the natural scale; and in the case of the imperfect, closed flowers, above described, if any new principle has to be '''invoked''', it must be one of retrogression rather than of progression; and so it must be with many parasitic and degraded animals.|year=1872|author=Charles Darwin|title=[[S:The Origin of Species/Chapter VII|The Origin of Species]]}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=It is easier to '''invoke''' or to deplore democracy than to say exactly what it is.|year=1912|author=William Sharp McKechnie|title=[[S:The New Democracy and the Constitution|The New Democracy and the Constitution]]}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[appeal]] for validation to a (notably cited) [[authority]].
#: {{ux|en|In certain Christian circles '''invoking''' the Bible constitutes irrefutable proof.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[conjure]] up with [[incantation]]s.
#: {{ux|en|This satanist ritual '''invokes''' Beelzebub.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bring about]] as an inevitable consequence.
#: {{ux|en|Blasphemy is taboo as it may '''invoke''' divine wrath.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[solicit]], petition for, [[appeal]] to a favorable attitude.
#: {{ux|en|The envoy '''invoked''' the King of Kings's magnanimity to reduce his province's tribute after another draught.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To cause (a [[program]] or [[subroutine]]) to execute.
#: {{ux|en|Interactive programs let the users enter choices and '''invoke''' the corresponding routines.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Stephen Prata|passage=C++ lets you '''invoke''' an operator function either by calling the function or by using the overloaded operator with its usual syntax.|year=2011|title=C++ Primer Plus}}

